{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>The strong of the flock</b> — what seems correct to me in this expression is that these are the flock whose males constantly pursue the females and never separate from them, because of their intense desire, after the manner of \"his soul is bound up with his soul\" (Genesis 44:30). And the language of the Sages is similar: \"a boar bound after a ewe\" — כָּרוּךְ אַחַר רָחֵל — in כִּרְכִּין אַחֲרֶיהָ (Bekhorot 24b; Kiddushin 79a). And the offspring born at that time are called \"the bound ones\" after the name of their fathers. Many say that \"bound\" (מְקֻשָּׁרוֹת) means the strong ones, whose limbs are firmly bound one to another, for this is the cause of health, while \"feeble\" (עֲטוּפִים) means the weak ones whose soul faints within them and who have no desire, after the manner of \"faint with hunger\" — הָעֲטוּפִים בְּרָעָב (Lamentations 2:19). And Onkelos rendered \"the firstborn and the weak ones\" — בַּכִּירַיָּא וְלַקִּישַׁיָּא — for so indeed the matter is."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:29.922176990Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}